# **9. SARSA vs. Q-Learning**

### **On-Policy (SARSA)**
- **Update Formula:**  
  \[
  Q(S_t, A_t) \;\leftarrow\; Q(S_t, A_t) + \alpha\,\bigl[R_{t+1} + \gamma\,Q(S_{t+1}, A_{t+1}) - Q(S_t, A_t)\bigr].
  \]
  - Here, the next action \(A_{t+1}\) is chosen from the **same policy** (e.g., \(\varepsilon\)-greedy).
- **On-Policy:** Learning occurs by **following** the same policy that is being evaluated and improved.

### **Off-Policy (Q-Learning)**
- **Update Formula:**  
  \[
  Q(S_t, A_t) \;\leftarrow\; Q(S_t, A_t) + \alpha\,\bigl[R_{t+1} + \gamma\,\max_{a'}Q(S_{t+1}, a') - Q(S_t, A_t)\bigr].
  \]
  - The next action used in the backup is the **best** action (maximum \(Q\)), even though in practice the agent may follow a different exploratory policy.
- **Off-Policy:** The optimal policy is learned while following a different behavior policy (e.g., \(\varepsilon\)-greedy).

### **Advantages and Disadvantages**
- **Q-Learning:**
  - Converges faster to the optimal policy (uses the \(\max\) in the next state).
  - More “aggressive,” which might cause issues if exploration is not adequate.
- **SARSA:**
  - More “conservative” (on-policy); convergence depends on the same exploratory policy.
  - Sometimes avoids risky situations (e.g., in some stochastic environments).

